            <img src="greenery-1.jpg" alt="greenery by Ashley Buchanan">
            <p> Environmental science is the study of how the environment
                and living organisms interact, and how human activities
                impact the environment. It's an interdisciplinary field
                that draws on many scientific disciplines, including biology,
                chemistry, geography, and physics.
            </p>

            <ul>Goals of environmental science

                <li>Learn how the natural world works</li>
                <li>Understand how humans impact the environment</li>
                <li>Develop ways to address environmental problems and live more sustainably</li>
            </ul>
            <ul>Environmental science subfields

                <li>Ecology: The study of how organisms interact with their environment</li>

                <li>Environmental chemistry: The study of the chemistry of the environment, including air and water
                    pollution</li>
                <li>Soil science: The study of soils and their role in the ecosystem</li>
                <li>Atmospheric sciences: The study of the atmosphere</li>
                <li>Geosciences: The study of the Earth's solid components, including energy and minerals</li>
            </ul>
            <ul>Environmental science careers

                <li>Atmospheric scientists or climatologists</li>
                <li>Environmental scientists and specialists</li>
                <li>Hydrologists</li>
                <li>Geoscientists</li>
                <li>Microbiologists</li>
                <li>Agriculture and food scientists</li>
                <li>Conservation scientists and foresters</li>
            </ul>
            <p>Environmental scientists collect, analyze, and report data to help inform policy and action.
            </p>
            <script src="main.js"></script>
    </body>

</html>